 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server authorizes each command, commits it as one event revision, and publishes that revision. Browsers hold a WebSocket connection to the project's own delivery service and display a revisioned view of the board.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Committed revisions reach connected browsers as they are published, without a realtime vendor and its recurring fees. The project owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect behavior in exchange. Three parts of the system follow from this:
+
+- **Change store** keeps every event revision in replayable order and publishes each committed revision to delivery reliably, so no committed edit is missing from the live stream.
+- **Delivery** holds the socket sessions, detects dead connections, and fans revisions out to browsers in the event.
+- **Browser sync** tracks the last revision it applied. After a disconnect it reconnects with backoff and requests the revisions it missed; if those revisions are no longer retained, it reloads the full board.
 
